this is the island of taiwan a  self-ruled democracy in the heart of the  pacific and tensions over taiwan are  higher than they've been in decades  that's because of how taiwan is viewed  around the world the u.s and its allies  support the island's strong democratic  system but the chinese communist party  insists that taiwan is part of china it  feels so strongly about this that its  leaders have vowed to take control of  taiwan by force if necessary things have  gotten so heated that people around the  world are starting to consider the  possibility of a conflict it is hard to  imagine um  that the taiwan could hold out if it  lost access to food electricity the  internet taking on china in a war is a  very big deal you will be a catastrophic  uh consequence if anything happens in  taiwan there are no indications that a  war is imminent but what could happen if  one actually broke out here's a look at  what a war could mean for the island the  pacific region and the entire world  first let's look at the island it's home  to 24 million people and at about 14 000  square miles it's less than one tenth  the size of california  it has eight major ports and four  international airports and those ports  help connect its high-tech economy to  the rest of the world this right here is  the taiwan strait about 100 miles across  it is mainland china in recent weeks  china staged military exercises in these  regions to show that it could isolate  taiwan  my colleague josh chin says the island's  terrain is both a blessing and a curse  for the people of taiwan taiwan is very  mountainous which is good in terms of  defending against an invasion  it's not so great in terms of arable  land that makes taiwan heavily dependent  on imports to survive since the island  only produces about a third of its food  and just 12 percent of its energy  another potential problem for taiwan is  that 95 percent of high-speed data is  transferred through cables that lie on  the ocean floor  so if china wanted to take the island  offline all it would have to do was use  submarines to cut those cables or launch  military strikes at the landing stations  where the cables come on land so we're  talking about critical shortages if a  war broke out and by the way taiwanese  forces would be greatly outnumbered  according to the pentagon china has more  than one million troops while taiwan has  just 88 000 that's why to successfully  defend taiwan the united states would  most likely have to step in are you  willing to get involved militarily to  defend taiwan if it comes to that yes  so let's zoom out a little wider what  would a conflict mean for the pacific a  war wouldn't just have implications for  the u.s military experts say it would  also affect u.s allies in the region  like japan korea the philippines and  australia to varying degrees the  japanese island of okinawa would play a  key role since it has major u.s military  facilities and is less than 500 miles  from taiwan the u.s territory of guam  would also be crucial since it's the  closest american soil to taiwan and  china and is home to major military  bases further south bases in australia  and the philippines could also  potentially come into play south korea  could also be affected but in a  different way because a conflict could  give north korean dictator kim jong-un  an incentive to launch an attack of his  own for that reason military experts i  talked to think it's unlikely that south  korea would directly participate in a  conflict some analysts believe it's  probably too hard to know who would win  a big war but a recent war game  conducted in washington found that the  u.s and taiwan can defend against a  chinese invasion mark cancion and the  center for strategic and international  studies hosted that war game so mark in  your simulation loss of life would be  significant we haven't done the  calculations yet about exactly what  numbers are but you'd be into the  certainly thousand twenty thousand  casualties casualties and equipment  losses that we haven't seen since the  second world war the war game found that  multi-billion dollar aircraft carriers  would be sunk hundreds of fighter jets  would be destroyed and key military  bases would be attacked while a  successful defense of taiwan could  strengthen u.s position in the pacific  mark says that big picture those kinds  of losses would dent u.s power around  the world it would take many years for  the u.s to completely rebuild  its forces after those kinds of losses  you know that might endanger the u.s  position in other parts of the world in  europe or the middle east so that would  be a tremendous risk as for taiwan there  would be a great deal of rebuilding to  do after such a costly conflict so let's  zoom out even more to look at how  devastating a conflict over taiwan would  be to the global economy  this tiny island plays a huge role in  international business just take a look  at this map of ship traffic in the  taiwan strait 90 of the world's largest  ships sailed through last year the  conflict would most likely shut it down  so you can imagine how that would  complicate the global supply chain even  worse a war could [ __ ] one of the  world's most critical industries  semiconductor manufacturing if bad  things happen all of a sudden  we're not talking about chip shortage  anymore the world loses 90 of that  manufacturing capacity taiwanese  companies dominate the industry  especially taiwan semiconductor  manufacturing company or tsmc their  biggest client apple today taiwan makes  70  of the world's microchips and they are  key to getting our cell phones computers  and even our cars to run smoothly  charles also says chips are needed to  develop and run software so do you think  we could lose our ability to tick-tock  or youtube or zoom if there were some  kind of conflict yes i can tell you that  a lot of vacations you love you feel  like it's part of your everyday life  they're gone  there are all kinds of products and  innovations that a chip crisis would  just derail electronics but also things  like healthcare products and renewable  energy parts in the event of a conflict  we may not be able to get any of them so  what would it cost to make taiwan's  semiconductors somewhere else charles  and other analysts i talked to say it  would take more than 300 billion dollars  and at least three years just to  replicate taiwan's semiconductor  industry clearly a war would come at a  heavy cost for taiwan the pacific and  the global economy and that includes  china which is also dependent on  taiwanese semiconductors and trade just  like the rest of the world
 

